<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The macOS keys that the whole reason for this app hangs on.

  `docs/deploy/networking.md` records the evening this project lost to it: on
  macOS 26 local-network access is granted per application, a process started by
  launchd has no grant and no way to ask for one, and the symptom is
  `EHOSTUNREACH` from the same Node binary that reaches the collector perfectly
  from Terminal. The deployed workaround is an SSH tunnel so the executor only
  ever talks to loopback, which is not gated.

  An app bundle CAN ask. That is the bet this app is: give the fleet a real
  bundle identity, let macOS put a dialog in front of a human, and the tunnel
  stops being load-bearing.

  WHAT IS UNVERIFIED, and it is the whole bet: the thing that opens the sockets
  is not this bundle, it is `fleet`, spawned as a Tauri sidecar -- a separate
  posix process whose executable happens to live inside Fleet.app/Contents/
  MacOS/. Whether macOS attributes its local-network traffic to this bundle's
  grant or treats it as an unattributed process (the launchd case again, with
  extra steps) has NOT been tested. See desktop/README.md, "The bet".

  Tauri's bundler merges this file into the generated Info.plist on macOS.
  Confirm that on the first build -- `plutil -p Fleet.app/Contents/Info.plist`
  -- because a key that is silently dropped looks exactly like a key that is
  present and ignored.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Shown verbatim in the permission dialog. Say what breaks without it,
       because "Fleet would like to find devices on your local network" tells a
       person nothing about whether saying no costs them anything. -->
  <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Fleet needs the local network to reach collectors and runner devices on this LAN. Without it, only a collector on this same machine will work.</string>

  <!-- Bonjour browsing is a separate gate from raw local-network access, and
       an app that is granted the network but has not declared the service type
       still finds nothing. `_fleet._tcp` is what collector/src/discovery.ts
       advertises and browses for; both directions are declared because this
       machine may be a brain, a runner, or -- the case the config's role list
       exists for -- both at once. -->
  <key>NSBonjourServices</key>
  <array>
    <string>_fleet._tcp</string>
  </array>

  <!-- The dashboard window loads http://127.0.0.1:<port>/dash in a WKWebView.
       Loopback is exempt from App Transport Security already; this key is for
       the case the settings window points the agent at `http://brain.local:8788`
       and somebody opens that instead. Without it WKWebView refuses the plain
       -HTTP load and reports it as an ordinary network failure. -->
  <key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
  <dict>
    <key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>

  <!-- A menu-bar app with no Dock tile and no menu bar of its own. Set here as
       well as via ActivationPolicy::Accessory at runtime: the runtime call
       happens after the app has already been placed, so on its own it produces
       a Dock icon that appears and then vanishes. -->
  <key>LSUIElement</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
